<%- include('layout-start.ejs', { title: 'Configuration — Functions', basePath, activeNav: 'getting-started' }) %>
<div class="main-container">
    <div class="getting-started-layout">
        <%- include('sidebar-docs.ejs', { basePath, manualChapter: 2, configStep: 1, configSub: 'functions' }) %>
        <div class="getting-started-layout-main">
            <div class="full-width-section">
                <h1>Functions</h1>
                <p>A <strong>function</strong> is one decoder feature — lights, horn, coupling, a sound effect, and so on — shown as a named button with icons instead of a bare F number. Function maps and trains pick functions from a shared library.</p>
                <p>Most of the time you only need the built-in library when building a <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/function-maps.html">function map</a>. Use this page when nothing fits — even after searching for a generic icon — and you want to create your own.</p>

                <aside class="doc-tip-banner" role="note" aria-label="What a function is" id="functions">
                    <p><strong>What is a function?</strong> Each function has two pictures: one for <strong>off</strong> and one for <strong>on</strong>, so in Control you can see at a glance whether it is active.</p>
                    <p>Functions also have a behaviour type:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Latching</strong> — tap once to turn on, tap again to turn off. The feature stays in that state until you change it. Lights are usually latching: you turn them on for a while and leave them.</li>
                        <li><strong>Momentary</strong> — the feature is only on while you hold the button; release and it stops. Horns and many sound effects are momentary — you would not want them stuck on after a tap.</li>
                        <li><strong>Automatic (default)</strong> — If you are not sure what to choose, or have a function that does not fit into the other categories, choose automatic. How the function works will then depend on how you press the button: a short tap will latch the function on, while a long press will activate a momentary effect. This is how most DCC controllers work. This also supports more complex patterns that some manufacturers use.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <div class="doc-function-pair">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/function-lights-off.svg" alt="Lights off"/>
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/function-lights-on.svg" alt="Lights on"/>
                        <span class="doc-function-label">Lights — latching (off / on)</span>
                    </div>
                    <div class="doc-function-pair">
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/function-horn-off.svg" alt="Horn off"/>
                        <img src="<%= basePath %>/images/config/function-horn-on.svg" alt="Horn on"/>
                        <span class="doc-function-label">Horn — momentary (off / on)</span>
                    </div>
                </aside>

                <h2>Step 1: Add New Function</h2>
                <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="<%= panelsAppOrigin %>/functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open functions on the Panels DCC website</a></p>
                <p>Sign in if you are asked to. Open <strong>Functions</strong> (or use the link above). Click <strong>Add New Function</strong>.</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'Functions library — add new function' }) %>

                <h2>Step 2: Name and behaviour</h2>
                <p>Give the function a short, clear <strong>Name</strong> — the label you will see in Control and in the map picker.</p>
                <p>Choose a behaviour type:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Latching</strong> — for features that stay on until you turn them off (lights, sound engines, and similar).</li>
                    <li><strong>Momentary</strong> — for features that should only fire while you hold the button (horns, whistles, many effects).</li>
                    <li><strong>Automatic</strong> — the default when you are unsure; short tap latches, long press is momentary.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Step 3: Off and on icons</h2>
                <p>Supply a picture for the <strong>off</strong> state and one for the <strong>on</strong> state. Keep them simple and high-contrast so they stay readable on a throttle at arm’s length.</p>
                <p>Match the style of the built-in icons where you can — that way your custom function sits comfortably beside library ones on the same train.</p>
                <%- include('doc-screenshot-placeholder.ejs', { label: 'New function — name, type, and off/on icons' }) %>

                <h2>Step 4: Save</h2>
                <p>Save the function. It then appears in the picker when you edit a <a href="<%= basePath %>/configuration/function-maps.html">function map</a> or a train, so you can drop it onto any F number like any other library entry.</p>

                <%- include('getting-started-pager.ejs', {
                    pagerPrevHref: basePath + '/configuration/function-maps.html',
                    pagerPrevTitle: 'Function maps',
                    pagerNextHref: basePath + '/configuration/accessories.html',
                    pagerNextTitle: 'Accessories'
                }) %>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
<%- include('layout-end.ejs') %>
